        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_015yZW3q62BpzgDxhEXVcTsA",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point for Secrets and Paths\\ndescription: Both credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions centralize configuration at startup through a single composition point, preventing scattered, late-binding decisions throughout the codebase.\\ntimestamp: 2025-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - concept/Security-by-Design\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly describe single startup-time configuration points; the connection between them is direct and intentional in the architecture.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2026-01-14\\n---\\n\\nBoth credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions reflect a shared architectural principle: **resolve all external dependencies and configuration at a single point at startup, never allow scattered late-binding throughout the codebase.**\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] implements this for secrets: the Hub reads the Anthropic API key from a single, well-known source at startup and injects it only into the spawned child process environments. The Hub\u0027s own process environment never holds the key \u2014 it never gets the chance to leak it.\\n\\n[[runtime-paths]] implements this for file locations: every path the Hub touches at runtime \u2014 wiki root, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories \u2014 is resolved in exactly one place at startup, through a single path resolver that reads configuration, validates everything exists, and reports the effective value and source of each location. No other part of the codebase is allowed to rediscover locations on its own.\\n\\nThe common value is **immutability and composability**: once resolved at startup, both the credential set and the path set are downstream-consumed constants, not re-evaluated, re-located, or re-mutated by any agent or subsystem. This eliminates a whole class of injection and leakage vulnerabilities: you cannot accidentally pass the wrong key or the wrong path because there is no \\\u0022wrong path\\\u0022 branch in your code \u2014 the Hub has already decided at startup, and every component receives its already-composed environment.\\n\\nAdding a new runtime location or a new credential scope means extending the Hub\u0027s startup composition logic once, not scattering new configuration decisions across the agents that will use them.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
